“Never received services” (Therapy Experience).
Significance codes: <math id="S4.T5.m21" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{}^{***}p&lt;0.001" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">∗</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∗</mo><mo lspace="0em">∗</mo></mrow></msup><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0.001</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}^{***}p&lt;0.001</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.T5.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{}^{**}p&lt;0.01" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mmultiscripts><mi>p</mi><mprescripts></mprescripts><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>∗</mo><mo lspace="0em">⁣</mo><mo>∗</mo></mrow></mmultiscripts><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0.01</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}^{**}p&lt;0.01</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.T5.m23" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{}^{*}p&lt;0.05" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0.05</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}^{*}p&lt;0.05</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.T5.m24" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{}^{.}p&lt;0.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>.</mo></msup><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0.1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}^{.}p&lt;0.1</annotation></semantics></math>.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.2.2. </span>Demographic Influence</h4>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Participant demographics also influenced ratings, with experience undergoing therapy as a significant predictor. Participants with less than one year of experience consistently rated LLM-generated answers lower (<math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta=-1.52,p=0.001" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1.52</mn></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.001</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta=-1.52,p=0.001</annotation></semantics></math>), while intermediate levels of experience (1–10 years) showed trends toward lower ratings that were marginally significant.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Other demographic factors, including education, age, gender, and participants’ ability to distinguish therapist-written answers from LLM-generated answers (can distinguish), did not significantly predict ratings. These findings suggest that therapist experience is the most pronounced predictor of participant evaluations, while geographic and other demographic effects are less clear, given the limitations of our sample.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.2.3. </span>Interaction Effect</h4>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In addition, we observe significant interaction effects between LLM type and participants’ therapist experience on user rating. In other words, experience undergoing therapy changes how participants judge each LLM’s responses. As shown by Table <a href="#S4.T5" title="Table 5 ‣ 4.2.1. Model Comparison ‣ 4.2. User Ratings ‣ 4. Findings ‣ Can LLMs Address Mental Health Questions? A Comparison with Human Therapists" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, participants with more than 10 years of therapy experience rated Llama outputs more favorably (<math id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma=+2.56,p=0.006" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>2.56</mn></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.006</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma=+2.56,p=0.006</annotation></semantics></math>) compared to ChatGPT outputs rated by participants with no therapist experience, highlighting that the perceived quality of Llama responses increases with professional experience. In contrast, having less than one year of therapy experience has the reversed effect for ratings of Gemini responses (<math id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma=+1.75,p=0.002" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1.75</mn></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.002</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma=+1.75,p=0.002</annotation></semantics></math>), indicating that participants with limited therapy experience rated Gemini more positively than might be expected based on the main effect alone. Overall, these results suggest that while Llama is generally on par with ChatGPT, it is especially well-regarded among participants with long-term therapy experience.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3. </span>Therapist Ratings</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Distributions of therapist ratings for each dimension are shown in Figure <a href="#S4.F17" title="Figure 17 ‣ 4.3. Therapist Ratings ‣ 4. Findings ‣ Can LLMs Address Mental Health Questions? A Comparison with Human Therapists" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">17</span></a> - <a href="#S4.F17" title="Figure 17 ‣ 4.3. Therapist Ratings ‣ 4. Findings ‣ Can LLMs Address Mental Health Questions? A Comparison with Human Therapists" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">17</span></a>.
Using the same Wilcoxon signed-rank test we applied to patient ratings, we evaluated therapist ratings across all six dimensions. As shown in Table <a href="#S4.T6" title="Table 6 ‣ 4.3. Therapist Ratings ‣ 4. Findings ‣ Can LLMs Address Mental Health Questions? A Comparison with Human Therapists" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>, therapists generally rated LLM answers higher, with one-sided p-values indicating statistically significant differences. Therapists rated ChatGPT answers significantly higher than therapist answers across all six dimensions, with <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> values ranging from 0.0156 to 0.00116. Therapists consistently rated Gemini responses significantly higher across all six dimensions, particularly for clarity and professional acceptability (<math id="S4.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{1}&lt;0.001" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0.001</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{1}&lt;0.001</annotation></semantics></math>). Therapists also rated Llama responses higher for most dimensions, though the differences were smaller and only marginally significant for overall liking (<math id="S4.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{1}=0.0454" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.0454</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{1}=0.0454</annotation></semantics></math>). Although under a conservative Bonferroni correction with an adjusted threshold of <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&lt;0.001" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0.001</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&lt;0.001</annotation></semantics></math>, many of these results are no longer statistically significant, this correction is likely overly conservative and may obscure meaningful differences, and the general trends remain valid. These findings indicate that, from a professional perspective, LLM-generated responses are generally acceptable and often rated positively, with some variation across models and dimensions, similar to the trends observed in general participant ratings.</p>
</div>
